{"entryType":"UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)","primaryAccession":"P01555","secondaryAccessions":["Q56634","Q9JPV1"],"uniProtkbId":"CHTA_VIBCH","entryAudit":{"firstPublicDate":"1986-07-21","lastAnnotationUpdateDate":"2026-09-02","lastSequenceUpdateDate":"1986-10-23","entryVersion":173,"sequenceVersion":1},"annotationScore":5.0,"organism":{"scientificName":"Vibrio cholerae serotype O1 (strain ATCC 39315 / El Tor Inaba N16961)","taxonId":243277,"lineage":["Bacteria","Pseudomonadati","Pseudomonadota","Gammaproteobacteria","Vibrionales","Vibrionaceae","Vibrio"]},"proteinExistence":"1: Evidence at protein level","proteinDescription":{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"value":"Cholera enterotoxin subunit A"}},"alternativeNames":[{"fullName":{"value":"Cholera enterotoxin, A chain"}}],"contains":[{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"value":"Cholera enterotoxin subunit A1"},"ecNumbers":[{"value":"2.4.2.-"}]},"alternativeNames":[{"fullName":{"value":"Cholera enterotoxin A1 chain"}},{"fullName":{"value":"Cholera enterotoxin alpha chain"}},{"fullName":{"value":"NAD(+)--diphthamide ADP-ribosyltransferase"}}]},{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"value":"Cholera enterotoxin subunit A2"}},"alternativeNames":[{"fullName":{"value":"Cholera enterotoxin A2 chain"}},{"fullName":{"value":"Cholera enterotoxin gamma chain"}}]}],"flag":"Precursor"},"genes":[{"geneName":{"value":"ctxA"},"synonyms":[{"value":"toxA"}],"orderedLocusNames":[{"value":"VC_1457"}]}],"comments":[{"texts":[{"value":"The A1 chain catalyzes the ADP-ribosylation of Gs alpha, a GTP-binding regulatory protein, to activate the adenylate cyclase. This leads to an overproduction of cAMP and eventually to a hypersecretion of chloride and bicarbonate followed by water, resulting in the characteristic cholera stool. The A2 chain tethers A1 to the pentameric ring"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"16099990"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"3214"}],"value":"The holotoxin (choleragen) consists of a pentameric ring of B subunits whose central pore is occupied by the A subunit. The A subunit contains two chains, A1 and A2, linked by a disulfide bridge. Interaction with the host protein ARF6 causes a conformation change so that the enterotoxin subunit A1 can bind NAD and catalyze the ADP-ribosylation of the host Gs alpha"}],"commentType":"SUBUNIT"},{"commentType":"INTERACTION","interactions":[{"interactantOne":{"uniProtKBAccession":"P01555","intActId":"EBI-1038392"},"interactantTwo":{"uniProtKBAccession":"P01556","geneName":"ctxB","intActId":"EBI-1038383"},"numberOfExperiments":5,"organismDiffer":false}]},{"texts":[{"value":"The four C-terminal residues of the A2 chain occupy the central pore of the holotoxin. Deletion of these residues weakens the interaction between the A subunit and the B pentamer without impairing the pentamer formation"}],"commentType":"DOMAIN"},{"texts":[{"value":"After binding to gangliosides GM1 in lipid rafts, through the subunit B pentamer, the holotoxin and the gangliosides are internalized. The holotoxin remains bound to GM1 until arrival in the ER. The A subunit has previously been cleaved in the intestinal lumen but the A1 and A2 chains have remained associated. In the ER, the A subunit disulfide bridge is reduced, the A1 chain is unfolded by the PDI and disassembled from the rest of the toxin. Then, the membrane-associated ER oxidase ERO1 oxidizes PDI, which releases the unfolded A1 chain. The next step is the retrotranslocation of A1 into the cytosol. This might be mediated by the protein-conducting pore SEC61. Upon arrival in the cytosol, A1 refolds and avoids proteasome degradation. In one way or another, A1 finally reaches its target and induces toxicity"}],"commentType":"MISCELLANEOUS"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305"}],"value":"Belongs to the enterotoxin A family"}],"commentType":"SIMILARITY"}],"features":[{"type":"Signal","location":{"start":{"value":1,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":18,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"7238869"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"955672"}]},{"type":"Chain","location":{"start":{"value":19,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":212,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Cholera enterotoxin subunit A1","featureId":"PRO_0000019342"},{"type":"Chain","location":{"start":{"value":213,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":258,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Cholera enterotoxin subunit A2","featureId":"PRO_0000019343"},{"type":"Active site","location":{"start":{"value":130,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":130,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000250"}]},{"type":"Binding site","location":{"start":{"value":25,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":28,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"","featureCrossReferences":[{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:57540"}],"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"16099990"}],"ligand":{"name":"NAD(+)","id":"ChEBI:CHEBI:57540"}},{"type":"Binding site","location":{"start":{"value":41,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":43,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"","featureCrossReferences":[{"database":"ChEBI","id":"CHEBI:57540"}],"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"16099990"}],"ligand":{"name":"NAD(+)","id":"ChEBI:CHEBI:57540"}},{"type":"Disulfide bond","location":{"start":{"value":205,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":217,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Interchain (between A1 and A2 chains)"},{"type":"Sequence conflict","location":{"start":{"value":20,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":20,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"in Ref. 9; AA sequence","evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305"}],"alternativeSequence":{"originalSequence":"D","alternativeSequences":["N"]}},{"type":"Sequence conflict","location":{"start":{"value":37,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":37,"modifier":"EXACT"}},